LEAD = {
    "headline": "THE SAME AD WAS REMEMBERED BY 41 PERCENT OF VIEWERS IN A LIGHT AD BREAK AND 26 PERCENT IN A HEAVY ONE.  NOBODY CHANGED THE AD",
    "deck": "FreeWheel tested 436 people on identical thirty-second spots inside the same streaming programme.  In the comparison that matters, the only thing changed was how many other ads sat beside the spot.  Recall fell by more than a third.  Every conversation you have ever had about why a film underperformed assumed the film was the variable, and inside one ad break it is not.",
    "stamps": [
        ("PPC LAND · 18 SEP", "https://ppc.land/more-ads-cut-streaming-brand-recall-from-41-to-26-freewheel-finds/"),
    ],
    "body": [
        "FreeWheel, the advertising technology arm of Comcast, ran this with MediaScience, a research firm that does lab-based advertising testing.  <mark>436 participants, in lab and in-home settings, watching thirty-second ads inside a thirty-minute streaming programme in a plain interface with no channel branding on it.</mark>  They measured with eye tracking and a survey afterwards.",
        "Two ad loads were tested.  Ad load is simply how many minutes of advertising sit inside a programme.  <mark>The heavy condition carried 9 minutes of ads per 30 minutes of programme, which is 30 percent of the runtime.  The light condition carried 3 minutes 45 seconds, which is 12.5 percent.</mark>",
        "<mark>Unaided recall — the share of people who could name the brand later with no prompt — came out at 41 percent on the light load and 26 percent on the heavy one.</mark>  Same ads.  Same length.  Same programme.  In this test, the fifteen-point gap came from nothing but the number of other advertisers in the break.",
        "Now do the arithmetic that nobody in the release does, because it is the whole decision.  <mark>Cutting from 9 minutes to 3 minutes 45 costs the publisher 5 minutes 15 seconds, which is 58 percent of its sellable ad time.</mark>  What the buyer collects for it is recall going from 26 to 41 percent.  Both figures are ours, from the two loads FreeWheel tested.  The two 58s are different quantities and the match between them is a coincidence of where the loads happened to be set, so do not build anything on the symmetry.  Build on the shape: the seller pays in inventory and the buyer gets paid in memory.",
        "That is why light-load inventory barely exists.  Cut the break by 58 percent and the publisher has 42 percent of its slots left, so it needs roughly two and a half times the price just to stand still.  You are buying one slot either way, so the same trade costs you nothing.  <mark>You are not choosing between good streaming inventory and bad streaming inventory.  You are choosing a load tier, and it is almost never on the rate card.</mark>",
        "The study carries other findings and they are worth separating rather than stacking.  A single exposure in streaming produced 33 percent unaided recall against 12 percent in social feeds.  Two streaming exposures produced 53 percent, two social exposures 24 percent, and one of each 42 percent.  On eye tracking, 94 percent of people looked at the streaming ads against 75 percent in social, with dwell time at 71 percent of the ad's duration against 52 percent.  Handle that streaming-beats-social comparison with tongs — see the flagnote — but the mixed result is the interesting one, because a single social exposure added to a single streaming exposure got most of the way to two streaming exposures.",
    ],
    "numbers": [
        ("41 / 26", "percent unaided recall for the identical ad in a light versus a heavy ad break"),
        ("58%", "of its sellable ad time a publisher gives up to deliver the lighter load — our arithmetic from the two loads tested"),
        ("436", "people tested by FreeWheel and MediaScience across lab and in-home settings"),
    ],
    "flagnote": "FreeWheel is owned by Comcast and its revenue depends on streaming advertising.  The deck is titled Why Brands Need TV and concludes that streaming beats social, so treat the streaming-versus-social comparison as a sales argument.  The ad-load finding is the credible part precisely because it cuts against the interests of FreeWheel's own publisher clients, who sell the heavier load.  The study is dated 15 September and was distributed on the 17th; we have found only one outlet covering the 41-to-26 figure, so it rests on a single read of a vendor study.  No fieldwork dates, no per-condition sample sizes and no confidence intervals were published.  PPC Land notes the deck discloses less about its eye-tracking method than it should, no co-viewing was accounted for, and no linear television was tested despite the title.  A separate FreeWheel study with a different research partner, surfaced the same week, reported that six minutes of ads per hour delivered 89 percent higher recall than twelve minutes per hour — same direction, different study, different method, and not to be merged with the numbers above.",
    "so_what": "Two ads competing for the same memory interfere with each other, and the more of them there are in a single break the less well any one of them gets stored.  That is not a creative problem and no amount of craft fixes it, which is uncomfortable because craft is the only lever most of us actually control.  The lever that moved the number by more than a third sits in the media plan, in a field that usually is not even filled in.",
    "do_this": "On your next streaming or connected television buy — connected television meaning ads served to a TV set over the internet rather than broadcast — ask the seller in writing what the ad load is on the inventory being offered, in minutes per half hour, and insist on a range rather than a single average.  Then compare your two best quotes on cost per thousand people reached adjusted for that load, and be willing to pay more for the lighter one.",
}

